1
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:22,044
[SPEAKER_04]: So, this is Samyuttanikāya 35, 246, the simile of the lute.

2
00:00:23,125 --> 00:00:23,585
[SPEAKER_04]: Bhikkhus.

3
00:00:23,865 --> 00:00:34,852
[SPEAKER_04]: If for any bhikkhu or bhikkhuni, desire or passion or aversion or muddledness or resistance in regard to sights cognizable by the eye arises, they should rein their mind in from that.

4
00:00:35,433 --> 00:00:38,615
[SPEAKER_04]: This path is dangerous and perilous, thorny and tangled.

5
00:00:38,795 --> 00:00:41,737
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a wrong turn, a bad path, a harmful way.

6
00:00:42,562 --> 00:00:46,043
[SPEAKER_04]: This path is frequented by untrue persons, not by true persons.

7
00:00:46,564 --> 00:00:47,604
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not worthy of you.

8
00:00:48,104 --> 00:00:51,866
[SPEAKER_04]: Therefore, they should reign in the mind from those sights cognizable by the eye.

9
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:59,969
[SPEAKER_04]: If for any bhikkhu or bhikkhuni desire or passion or aversion or muddledness or resistance in regard to sounds cognizable by the ear,

10
00:01:00,990 --> 00:01:04,171
[SPEAKER_04]: This path is dangerous and perilous, thorny and tangled.

11
00:01:04,191 --> 00:01:06,231
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a wrong turn, a bad path, a harmful way.

12
00:01:06,251 --> 00:01:08,472
[SPEAKER_04]: This path is frequented by untrue persons, not by true persons.

13
00:01:24,537 --> 00:01:25,478
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not worthy of you.

14
00:01:26,098 --> 00:01:31,102
[SPEAKER_04]: Therefore, they should rein in the mind from those phenomena cognizable by the mind.

15
00:01:32,143 --> 00:01:35,325
[SPEAKER_04]: Suppose the crops have ripened, but the caretaker is negligent.

16
00:01:35,785 --> 00:01:40,328
[SPEAKER_04]: If an ox fond of crops invades the crops, they'd indulge themselves as much as they like.

17
00:01:41,029 --> 00:01:53,678
[SPEAKER_04]: In the same way, an unlearned ordinary person, failing to exercise restraint in regard to the six fields of pressure, indulges themselves as much as they like and falls into carelessness in regard to the five strands of sensuality.

18
00:01:54,635 --> 00:01:57,576
[SPEAKER_04]: Suppose the crops have ripened and the caretaker is diligent.

19
00:01:58,016 --> 00:02:02,736
[SPEAKER_04]: If an ox fond of crops invades the crops, the caretaker would grab them firmly by the muzzle.

20
00:02:03,196 --> 00:02:05,897
[SPEAKER_04]: They'd grab them above the hump and hold them fast there.

21
00:02:06,597 --> 00:02:09,138
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, they'd give them a good thrashing before driving them away.

22
00:02:09,878 --> 00:02:13,178
[SPEAKER_04]: A second time, and even a third time, the same thing might happen.

23
00:02:13,738 --> 00:02:23,180
[SPEAKER_04]: As a result, no matter how long they stand or sit in a village or wilderness, that ox fond of crops would never invade that crop again, remembering the beating they got earlier.

24
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:31,520
[SPEAKER_04]: In the same way, when a bhikkhu's mind is subdued, well subdued in regard to the six fields of pressure, it becomes established internally.

25
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:33,983
[SPEAKER_04]: It settles, unifies, and becomes composed.

26
00:02:35,527 --> 00:02:39,049
[SPEAKER_04]: Suppose a king or their minister had never heard the sound of a lute.

27
00:02:39,589 --> 00:02:48,054
[SPEAKER_04]: When he first hears the sound, he'd say, my man, what is making this sound so arousing, sensuous, intoxicating, infatuating, and captivating?

28
00:02:48,635 --> 00:02:50,896
[SPEAKER_04]: They'd say to him, that, sir, is a lute.

29
00:02:51,396 --> 00:02:53,358
[SPEAKER_04]: He'd say, go, my man, fetch me that lute.

30
00:02:53,938 --> 00:03:00,462
[SPEAKER_04]: So they'd fetch it and say, this, sir, is that lute whose sound is so arousing, sensuous, intoxicating, infatuating, and captivating.

31
00:03:01,042 --> 00:03:03,223
[SPEAKER_04]: He'd say, I'd had enough of that lute.

32
00:03:03,623 --> 00:03:04,804
[SPEAKER_04]: Just fetch me the sound.

33
00:03:05,384 --> 00:03:10,707
[SPEAKER_04]: They'd say, sir, this lute is made of many components assembled together, which make a sound when they're played.

34
00:03:11,287 --> 00:03:18,690
[SPEAKER_04]: That is, it depends on the body, the skin, the neck, the head, the strings, the plectrum, and a person to play it properly.

35
00:03:19,171 --> 00:03:23,793
[SPEAKER_04]: That's how a lute is made of many components assembled together, which make a sound when they're played.

36
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:28,683
[SPEAKER_04]: But he'd split that loot into ten pieces or a hundred pieces, then splinter it up.

37
00:03:29,163 --> 00:03:31,905
[SPEAKER_04]: He'd burn the splinters with fire and reduce them to ashes.

38
00:03:32,606 --> 00:03:36,929
[SPEAKER_04]: Then he'd sweep away the ashes in a strong wind, or float them away down a swift stream.

39
00:03:37,449 --> 00:03:42,353
[SPEAKER_04]: And he'd say, a poor thing indeed is this so-called loot, and whatever else is called a loot.

40
00:03:42,893 --> 00:03:45,695
[SPEAKER_04]: But people waste their time with it, careless and duped.

41
00:03:46,436 --> 00:03:49,798
[SPEAKER_04]: In the same way, a bhikkhu investigates form, as far as it goes.

42
00:03:50,359 --> 00:03:51,660
[SPEAKER_04]: Feeling, as far as it goes.

43
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:53,481
[SPEAKER_04]: Perception, as far as it goes.

44
00:03:53,965 --> 00:03:57,447
[SPEAKER_04]: Activities as far as they go, and consciousness as far as it goes.

45
00:03:58,128 --> 00:04:03,351
[SPEAKER_04]: As he investigates them in this way, any ideas of I, or mine, or I am, do not occur to him.

46
00:04:09,435 --> 00:04:09,995
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

47
00:04:11,676 --> 00:04:18,881
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a prime example of a practice of samatha first, and vipassana later.

48
00:04:21,185 --> 00:04:37,472
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a prevalent notion that samatha and vipassana are two separate things, or two separate techniques, let's say, two separate types of meditation.

49
00:04:38,373 --> 00:04:42,594
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, the suttas don't really support that idea.

50
00:04:43,635 --> 00:04:48,237
[SPEAKER_01]: But, to the extent that you could argue that there is such a difference,

51
00:04:49,571 --> 00:05:06,156
[SPEAKER_01]: It's exemplified by this sort of practice, where somebody is concerned with directly abandoning, as it says there, passion, aversion, muddledness, resistance, desire, essentially the five hindrances in relation to the six senses.

52
00:05:07,256 --> 00:05:12,238
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the samatha aspect.

53
00:05:13,238 --> 00:05:15,259
[SPEAKER_01]: calming down defilements.

54
00:05:16,159 --> 00:05:29,783
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, once the mind is calm, as it says there, you're then in a position to investigate more fundamental aspects of your experience, like the the five aggregates.

55
00:05:30,870 --> 00:05:44,999
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the suttas, it's said sometimes, like, some person might have a more of a tendency for vipassana, and then they should strive to attain samatha, or they have more of a tendency for samatha, and they should strive

56
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:49,003
[SPEAKER_01]: to gain vipassana, to gain insight.

57
00:05:49,483 --> 00:05:50,424
[SPEAKER_01]: It literally says that, actually.

58
00:05:50,444 --> 00:05:53,067
[SPEAKER_01]: It says gain, which means it's a quality.

59
00:05:53,787 --> 00:05:55,289
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a technique or a method.

60
00:05:55,809 --> 00:05:57,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Samatha is the quality of calm.

61
00:05:57,891 --> 00:05:59,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Vipassana is the quality of insight.

62
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:01,294
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're not things you do.

63
00:06:02,415 --> 00:06:03,836
[SPEAKER_01]: They are qualities.

64
00:06:04,237 --> 00:06:04,657
[SPEAKER_01]: And so,

65
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:21,001
[SPEAKER_01]: You can see that there's a very big difference between this, which is a... like, it clearly requires discernment, to some extent, to do this practice of reflecting on the danger of unwholesome states.

66
00:06:21,802 --> 00:06:27,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, incidentally, the only thing that the suttas teach as a method to abandon hindrances.

67
00:06:27,826 --> 00:06:34,411
[SPEAKER_01]: There isn't a single mention of absorption method that leads you to samatha.

68
00:06:34,591 --> 00:06:41,857
[SPEAKER_01]: The samatha method of the suttas is contemplating the danger in the five hindrances, and that occurs like dozens of times.

69
00:06:42,617 --> 00:06:53,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have suttas like these, and you have suttas like those two types of thoughts, Majjhima Nikāya 19, which are basically talking about the same principle, just with like slightly different terms.

70
00:06:53,881 --> 00:07:02,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Like this one talking about the six senses, the other one talks about the three types of thought, but fundamentally, all of it is about calming the unwholesome states.

71
00:07:02,825 --> 00:07:03,565
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you calm.

72
00:07:04,165 --> 00:07:10,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you have just absorption or concentration practice, what you do is like you calm everything.

73
00:07:11,642 --> 00:07:21,909
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like you imagine you have six people, and three of them committed a crime, but you throw all of them in jail.

74
00:07:23,230 --> 00:07:27,914
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically what's happening with the contemporary idea of a Samatha practice.

75
00:07:28,835 --> 00:07:32,477
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't actually want to see what rediversion and delusion is.

76
00:07:33,118 --> 00:07:34,238
[SPEAKER_01]: You just want to calm everything.

77
00:07:34,879 --> 00:07:37,181
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so does that even qualify?

78
00:07:37,221 --> 00:07:40,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Because are these five hindrances on that level?

79
00:07:40,772 --> 00:07:41,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

80
00:07:41,192 --> 00:07:52,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the thing is, that attempt to just come everything indiscriminately, that's also driven by pre-diversion and delusion, ultimately.

81
00:07:52,304 --> 00:07:57,269
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, then, the states that result from that, if you read in between the lines, you see that there's still craving there, because

82
00:07:57,910 --> 00:08:06,614
[SPEAKER_01]: There's mentions of, like, delighting the pleasure and, you know, indulging in the bodily sensations or whatever.

83
00:08:06,794 --> 00:08:16,838
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's precisely because that effort, that initial effort, that would have led to the right type of calm, to actually discern what was unwholesome from the very start.

84
00:08:17,098 --> 00:08:18,098
[SPEAKER_01]: That effort was never made.

85
00:08:19,859 --> 00:08:23,841
[SPEAKER_01]: People just took up a method and then did it until this result came about.

86
00:08:24,361 --> 00:08:25,882
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was no discernment of

87
00:08:26,922 --> 00:08:28,323
[SPEAKER_01]: What actually the five hindrances are.

88
00:08:28,923 --> 00:08:33,186
[SPEAKER_01]: And the suttas even say there isn't, like...

89
00:08:33,226 --> 00:08:44,413
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, not that jhāna is explicitly samatha, but you could argue, like, sort of... standard form of samatha is jhāna.

90
00:08:44,873 --> 00:08:49,516
[SPEAKER_01]: And the suttas say there is no jhāna without wisdom, and there is no wisdom without jhāna.

91
00:08:50,296 --> 00:08:55,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it does talk about samatha and some other sutta as unification and as composure.

92
00:08:55,498 --> 00:08:57,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's clearly moving in that same direction.

93
00:08:57,619 --> 00:08:57,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

94
00:08:58,199 --> 00:09:07,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says, like, even the first jhāna, if you don't understand the danger in sensuality, and you can generalize it for any unwholesome states, you won't be able to attain it.

95
00:09:07,743 --> 00:09:18,628
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how much you can absorb yourself, because you can see that that sort of contemplation, based on that danger that you understood, that's the tool you're using, like in all these suttas.

96
00:09:19,548 --> 00:09:20,389
[SPEAKER_01]: To calm the mind.

97
00:09:21,250 --> 00:09:47,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, what comes from that is, when the mind is withdrawn from the hindrances, most classically described in the first jhāna formula, where it's separated from unwholesome states, then you're in a position to contemplate these things, because... not because you have, like, increased powers of observation, of, like, minute perceptions that now arise due to your absorption,

98
00:09:48,415 --> 00:09:49,395
[SPEAKER_01]: But, quite the opposite.

99
00:09:49,655 --> 00:09:53,037
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like you, sort of, looking at everything from a helicopter now.

100
00:09:53,837 --> 00:09:56,098
[SPEAKER_01]: You can look at, you can survey the territory.

101
00:09:56,318 --> 00:09:59,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it says, he examines form as far as form goes.

102
00:10:00,580 --> 00:10:01,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not...

103
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:11,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's talking about a generality of a broader picture, not of a... not like you look at it through a microscope, and then you go back to your normal state, and what does that help you with?

104
00:10:12,224 --> 00:10:16,886
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you, if you look at everything from above, as it says, separated from these things,

105
00:10:17,466 --> 00:10:33,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, eventually, when you come out, when you go back to your normal state, and you're, like, on the ground, then you have the memory of, like, you know, what you saw, and you know where to go, and you will never be able to get lost in that territory.

106
00:10:33,962 --> 00:10:35,103
[SPEAKER_00]: In particularities, then.

107
00:10:35,343 --> 00:10:35,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

108
00:10:35,944 --> 00:10:53,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, that's how practicing samatha would lead to permanent, which is just a temporary liberation, would lead to permanent liberation, even when you don't have this actual, like, bodily freedom and peace and so on.

109
00:10:54,702 --> 00:11:00,908
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, an arahant wouldn't need to practice samādhi anymore, as it's said to say, it would just be a pleasant, for the sake of a pleasant abiding.

110
00:11:01,368 --> 00:11:21,400
[SPEAKER_01]: But they don't need to do it anymore, if they don't want to, because they already saw, became so familiar with that territory from above, by having looked at it from above, that, yeah, they can navigate through without ever inducing ignorance, like I said, getting lost in, like, not knowing where they are, doubt and so on.

111
00:11:25,023 --> 00:11:25,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

112
00:11:28,034 --> 00:11:33,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are there any prerequisites for getting the samatha right?

113
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:38,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Can someone just, out of the blue, start reflecting on the dangers of sensuality?

114
00:11:40,367 --> 00:11:42,127
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, are there ways of doing that wrong?

115
00:11:42,528 --> 00:11:46,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Or can you never go wrong contemplating the danger of sensuality?

116
00:11:46,328 --> 00:11:49,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Which basically means that your starting point for the samatha would always be right.

117
00:11:49,949 --> 00:11:50,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

118
00:11:50,209 --> 00:11:55,591
[SPEAKER_01]: So, definitely, it would be better to do that than to do most things people do.

119
00:11:56,191 --> 00:12:05,693
[SPEAKER_01]: But, of course, it would not be a genuine attempt of any kind if in your daily life you haven't abandoned sensuality.

120
00:12:06,213 --> 00:12:09,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, as it says, the prerequisite

121
00:12:10,215 --> 00:12:19,130
[SPEAKER_01]: for the first jhāna is he has understood as it really is, not just intellectually or abstractly, like, you know, these things...

122
00:12:21,124 --> 00:12:25,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Just an idea of all these things are not satisfactory or something that everybody can have.

123
00:12:25,207 --> 00:12:25,327
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

124
00:12:25,387 --> 00:12:29,191
[SPEAKER_01]: He has understood, as it really is, the danger in these things.

125
00:12:29,591 --> 00:12:40,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can ask yourself, like, yes, I'm now trying to practice meditation, but in my broader life, do I still have a plan to, like, go back to these things on a physical and verbal level?

126
00:12:41,661 --> 00:12:48,327
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer is yes, then you can be quite clear that you don't see really that danger, because you would never want to go back to something that's dangerous.

127
00:12:49,387 --> 00:12:49,867
[SPEAKER_01]: To yourself.

128
00:12:49,988 --> 00:13:02,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you still want to go back, it's because, yeah, you might have an abstract idea of the danger, but you haven't seen it in that concrete sense that would induce that disenchantment that this talk is about.

129
00:13:02,194 --> 00:13:11,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Where, like, somebody tries to throw you into this charcoal pit, and you just refuse in any... you would not consent to that.

130
00:13:11,538 --> 00:13:11,859
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

131
00:13:12,759 --> 00:13:23,169
[SPEAKER_01]: And, of course, another thing is that samatha-vipassana is basically the cultivation of the seven factors of enlightenment or of awakening.

132
00:13:23,389 --> 00:13:27,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It's said in the suttas that these factors are acquired with the right view.

133
00:13:28,555 --> 00:13:28,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

134
00:13:28,995 --> 00:13:30,436
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, eh... that's, eh...

135
00:13:30,616 --> 00:13:35,359
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, Sankhya 4630, or something, or 4730?

136
00:13:35,379 --> 00:13:39,742
[SPEAKER_00]: No, yeah, no, eh... yeah, no, it's 46, the Gojanga Summita Dayi.

137
00:13:39,782 --> 00:13:41,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, with Venerable Dayi.

138
00:13:41,203 --> 00:13:41,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

139
00:13:41,503 --> 00:13:57,353
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, he was contemplating the... it says, like, the turning around of the five aggregates, and then he finally this... he finally acquired the seven factors of enlightenment, meaning he... like, with the right view, together with the right view.

140
00:13:58,208 --> 00:14:11,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so that is actually not so frequently talked about, but just as one gains stream-entry or acquires the Noble Eightfold Path, these seven factors of enlightenment, or seven factors of awakening, they are exactly on that same level.

141
00:14:11,156 --> 00:14:11,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

142
00:14:11,596 --> 00:14:19,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just a full... it's fully synonymous, seven factors of enlightenment, with the Noble Eightfold Path.

143
00:14:19,281 --> 00:14:19,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

144
00:14:19,621 --> 00:14:22,423
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha in that Sutta Vidudayi also confirms that.

145
00:14:23,043 --> 00:14:27,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, he says, yes, these are indeed these factors that you have now acquired,

146
00:14:28,306 --> 00:14:36,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you then develop them, they will lead to full, complete abandonment of greed, hatred and delusion.

147
00:14:36,314 --> 00:14:36,474
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

148
00:14:37,755 --> 00:14:43,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so it's... it's... because, I mean, the first one is mindfulness, or sati, recollection.

149
00:14:43,941 --> 00:14:50,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as it's to say, there cannot be right recollection without right view, because that's what you recollect.

150
00:14:50,288 --> 00:14:52,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You recollect your understanding of what craving is.

151
00:14:52,990 --> 00:15:05,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And based on that, while you discern that craving in the experience, dhamma-vijñāya, and see, that's actually another thing that sort of ties in with how samatha cannot happen without vipassana.

152
00:15:05,332 --> 00:15:18,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Like dhamma-vijñāya, you could say like distinguishing or discernment of phenomena, comes before joy and tranquility and samādhi and everything else.

153
00:15:18,815 --> 00:15:20,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you cannot distinguish

154
00:15:21,735 --> 00:15:30,922
[SPEAKER_01]: In the most, in the broadest terms, like, what the problem actually is in this experience, then you will be calming down the wrong thing.

155
00:15:30,942 --> 00:15:35,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Like I said before, you're basically calming down, like, your sense objects.

156
00:15:35,365 --> 00:15:42,551
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see, as I said, the simile of the cook, that discussion we previously had about the sign of the mind.

157
00:15:43,151 --> 00:15:48,055
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see where that attitude or that defilement is that you need to abandon.

158
00:15:48,655 --> 00:15:52,617
[SPEAKER_01]: You just kind of go a roundabout way by sort of destroying the whole thing.

159
00:15:53,338 --> 00:16:10,848
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that doesn't count, really, because it's precisely because you become accustomed to discerning what greed, aversion and delusion is, and you get that confirmation that you did actually do it properly,

160
00:16:11,448 --> 00:16:13,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Out of the peace that comes from it.

161
00:16:13,090 --> 00:16:19,098
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how, then, your understanding of what these pre-division and division are becomes naturally fortified.

162
00:16:19,658 --> 00:16:29,911
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just get used to just abolishing everything, no matter how peaceful it is, you haven't really, like, gained any understanding of how to distinguish.

163
00:16:31,032 --> 00:16:32,393
[SPEAKER_01]: What is greed from non-greed?

164
00:16:32,593 --> 00:16:34,153
[SPEAKER_01]: What is aversion from non-aversion?

165
00:16:34,273 --> 00:16:35,514
[SPEAKER_00]: It's also not really maintainable.

166
00:16:35,554 --> 00:16:40,316
[SPEAKER_00]: If you continue, you have to basically destroy the whole experience, which is impossible in the sense as well.

167
00:16:40,436 --> 00:16:45,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so you're just sort of replacing one thing with another, but why that

168
00:16:46,998 --> 00:16:51,161
[SPEAKER_01]: What aspects were actually problematic, you never really understood.

169
00:16:54,164 --> 00:17:11,537
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, regarding the vipassana component, it's said there that it gives the simile of the lute, which doesn't mean that you see some reality that's hidden.

170
00:17:12,418 --> 00:17:15,440
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing that you had before.

171
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:21,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it says that same lute, but now you see, as it says there, its foundations.

172
00:17:21,885 --> 00:17:27,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, he focuses closer on the on the fabric of the lute, like the...

173
00:17:28,409 --> 00:17:36,475
[SPEAKER_01]: The word or something, it's it's a general sort of thing that enables, like it says, enables the sound.

174
00:17:36,495 --> 00:17:41,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Because initially, you're just focused on the the pleasant sound, as it says, that's gratifying, that's pleasant.

175
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:47,383
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't see what is the condition for this thing to be able to be there.

176
00:17:47,904 --> 00:17:53,247
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, then, what you get to see with samādhi is exactly pāṭicca-saṃpāda.

177
00:17:53,328 --> 00:17:54,689
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to see the dependence

178
00:17:55,569 --> 00:18:03,957
[SPEAKER_01]: of everything that you could have gained any gratification or pleasure from, you see that it's dependent on this foundation that's not in your control.

179
00:18:04,017 --> 00:18:06,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a simultaneous discernment.

180
00:18:07,300 --> 00:18:10,642
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like a sequence that, like, so fast

181
00:18:11,383 --> 00:18:20,747
[SPEAKER_01]: That you need to catch it with this increased observation, because what... how do you know that there isn't an even faster sequence that you're not seeing?

182
00:18:20,767 --> 00:18:21,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's endless, yeah.

183
00:18:21,928 --> 00:18:26,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but this is like, no, you just see the whole thing in its entirety.

184
00:18:27,090 --> 00:18:34,732
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, anything that you see, anything that could possibly arouse desire, is within this.

185
00:18:35,352 --> 00:18:36,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, this is the extent.

186
00:18:36,673 --> 00:18:38,553
[SPEAKER_01]: As far as it goes, this is what form is.

187
00:18:38,693 --> 00:18:40,334
[SPEAKER_01]: As far as it goes, this is what feeling is.

188
00:18:40,494 --> 00:18:41,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so it doesn't leave anything out.

189
00:18:41,934 --> 00:18:42,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

190
00:18:42,394 --> 00:18:43,754
[SPEAKER_00]: If that contemplation is done properly.

191
00:18:43,774 --> 00:18:45,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I remember also the sutta.

192
00:18:46,135 --> 00:19:01,783
[SPEAKER_00]: In the Anguttara-Bhagavad-Gita 4, it says if somebody has samatha, but he doesn't gain the vipassana, you should find someone who can show him what is now vipassana, and then you should ask that monk, like, you know, and then it talks specifically about the conditionality of a vision phenomenon.

193
00:19:01,823 --> 00:19:03,304
[SPEAKER_01]: How to examine saṅkhara, it says.

194
00:19:03,504 --> 00:19:03,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

195
00:19:04,204 --> 00:19:12,849
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the vipassana there is, as you just mentioned, entirely bound up with seeing dependent origination, which is already... which equals basically having the right view.

196
00:19:13,689 --> 00:19:19,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think it says somewhere else as well, like, samatha and vipassana pertain to true knowledge, right?

197
00:19:19,978 --> 00:19:25,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is something that one should aim at establishing in one's experience.

198
00:19:25,906 --> 00:19:27,749
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we're basically saying that

199
00:19:28,833 --> 00:19:35,337
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, to become a noble disciple is to gain the foundation for doing both the samatha and the vipassana rightly.

200
00:19:35,798 --> 00:19:42,102
[SPEAKER_00]: If that right view is not there, then that's not quite the calm that the suttas talk about when they talk about samatha.

201
00:19:42,703 --> 00:19:42,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Right?

202
00:19:43,063 --> 00:19:50,228
[SPEAKER_01]: And definitely, if you did gain, like, a significant degree of samatha, like, let's say, the first jhāna,

203
00:19:50,828 --> 00:19:52,689
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't have the right view.

204
00:19:53,549 --> 00:19:54,730
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a red flag right there.

205
00:19:55,030 --> 00:20:02,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, as they say, even a sotāpanna would hardly have reached that point yet.

206
00:20:02,714 --> 00:20:09,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like you have an even clearer, less defiled mind than a sotāpanna.

207
00:20:10,690 --> 00:20:17,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Buddha said, it's like a puddle that has fish in it, but then it's muddled, so you don't see actually what's inside.

208
00:20:18,120 --> 00:20:23,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, yours is clearer than Sotāpanna's mind, but you don't see the fish clearly.

209
00:20:23,807 --> 00:20:25,029
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see the Four Noble Truths.

210
00:20:25,970 --> 00:20:30,711
[SPEAKER_01]: You should really reconsider whether that's actually the right samatha.

211
00:20:31,211 --> 00:20:41,014
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... and another sutta, in fact, says, again feeding into how it's this... their qualities are not things you do.

212
00:20:41,034 --> 00:20:42,775
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, when you understand the I,

213
00:20:44,415 --> 00:20:56,509
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand the sights, you understand eye pressure or eye contact, you understand the feeling born of eye contact, you understand eye consciousness, and it repeats the same for the the other five senses.

214
00:20:57,742 --> 00:21:00,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it starts to outline the entire path.

215
00:21:00,924 --> 00:21:15,831
[SPEAKER_01]: When you have that, the four right strivings are present, the four bases of power are present, the seven enlightenment factors are present, the five faculties are present, and then it mentions samatha, vipassana as well.

216
00:21:16,331 --> 00:21:19,553
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't say that he does samatha, vipassana by like...

217
00:21:20,113 --> 00:21:37,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, him choosing, it's like these two things are there on account of him having this understanding beforehand of the six senses, which is another way of saying, one of the many ways of presenting what the right view is, the understanding of the six senses.

218
00:21:38,507 --> 00:21:42,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, really, it's more like... it's not even something you get to choose.

219
00:21:42,249 --> 00:21:46,393
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like some people could do the same contemplation

220
00:21:48,687 --> 00:21:59,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, they would be more naturally inclining towards using that contemplation to clear their mind of defilements.

221
00:22:00,287 --> 00:22:08,950
[SPEAKER_01]: But they might not go far enough to take it to the point of final knowledge or final insight.

222
00:22:09,430 --> 00:22:19,614
[SPEAKER_01]: On the other hand, some other people might have more of a tendency for that clarification and clarity of phenomena, but they have a little bit too much.

223
00:22:19,714 --> 00:22:21,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, that results in restlessness.

224
00:22:22,155 --> 00:22:35,598
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same contemplation, actually, because it doesn't... like, if you contemplate the five aggregates, it doesn't necessarily mean that you're going to be doing vipassana.

225
00:22:35,618 --> 00:22:39,219
[SPEAKER_01]: You could do that also as a way of calming down the mind.

226
00:22:39,239 --> 00:22:44,621
[SPEAKER_01]: You just contemplate, well, look, I'm having these desires, I'm having these pressures here,

227
00:22:45,561 --> 00:22:46,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this form actually mine?

228
00:22:47,041 --> 00:22:48,502
[SPEAKER_01]: This form that I'm craving for?

229
00:22:48,522 --> 00:22:51,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is this feeling that I'm resisting, is it actually mine?

230
00:22:51,543 --> 00:22:53,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, did I ask this thing to arise?

231
00:22:55,064 --> 00:23:02,026
[SPEAKER_01]: And you contemplate it in the five aggregates, but you only take it to the point where the the hindrances calm down, and you don't take it further.

232
00:23:02,086 --> 00:23:13,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's a person who, on account of what people would generally think is vipassana, is inclining more towards samatha, because it's just... it's not even something like you would be aware of

233
00:23:16,932 --> 00:23:22,940
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a natural inclination that each person would have.

234
00:23:23,400 --> 00:23:29,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm kind of doing the same practice of contemplation and mindfulness, which is the thing that binds everything together.

235
00:23:32,421 --> 00:23:43,064
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, in that sutta that we started out with, it says that by seeing the danger in, you know, greed, delusion, malevolence, and so on, the mind calms down, which is a samatha aspect.

236
00:23:43,544 --> 00:23:54,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps you can explore this a bit more now, because ordinarily, one would assume thinking about something which is dangerous would make one restless, anxious, maybe stir up the mind.

237
00:23:54,607 --> 00:23:59,389
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, there can be a natural tendency to not think about that which is dangerous, because you want to gain calm.

238
00:24:00,050 --> 00:24:05,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, the Buddha comes along and says the mind calms down precisely when you contemplate the danger in those things.

239
00:24:06,605 --> 00:24:09,392
[SPEAKER_00]: So, perhaps it's worthwhile understanding why that is so.

240
00:24:11,439 --> 00:24:15,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean, there's endless suttas about it, how that's what leads to it.

241
00:24:15,581 --> 00:24:31,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, so how would you go about... because it can sound to most people like it's just a sort of abstract philosophical mantra, like, oh, this is dangerous, this is dangerous, and clearly that won't do much.

242
00:24:32,210 --> 00:24:37,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is it that... how is it that you contemplate the danger, let's say?

243
00:24:47,355 --> 00:24:53,848
[SPEAKER_01]: And what does it mean when it says there, you rein the mind in, away from these things?

244
00:24:53,888 --> 00:24:57,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it mean, like, you completely just delete them from your awareness?

245
00:24:58,035 --> 00:24:59,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is there something more to that?

246
00:25:15,581 --> 00:25:16,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, let's go into that.

247
00:25:16,462 --> 00:25:18,465
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it mean to reign in the mind?

248
00:25:20,027 --> 00:25:21,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, practically speaking.

249
00:25:21,368 --> 00:25:25,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, from sense objects, which is specifically what it's talking about there.

250
00:25:25,433 --> 00:25:26,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, where do you go?

251
00:25:27,996 --> 00:25:28,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Right?

252
00:25:28,557 --> 00:25:30,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not that sense objects disappear.

253
00:25:30,940 --> 00:25:33,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, can you decide to make sense objects disappear?

254
00:25:33,381 --> 00:25:35,502
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be almost like disappointing your whole experience.

255
00:25:35,782 --> 00:25:38,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can, but the thing is, the sutta is explicitly described.

256
00:25:39,524 --> 00:25:47,107
[SPEAKER_01]: In jhāna, you walk, sit, lie down, and it says it's an escape amidst the confinement of the five senses.

257
00:25:47,127 --> 00:25:48,968
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't, like, stop the five senses.

258
00:25:50,529 --> 00:25:50,749
[SPEAKER_04]: Right.

259
00:25:50,769 --> 00:25:54,370
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I'm thinking, well, you're reeling the mind back.

260
00:25:54,950 --> 00:26:00,173
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, it... the fact that it says mind, you have to...

261
00:26:00,975 --> 00:26:06,299
[SPEAKER_04]: Bear in mind that the mind is not the kind of the thing you're looking at.

262
00:26:06,359 --> 00:26:08,240
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not reel your attention back.

263
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:13,944
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, from looking at this thing out there, like, okay, I need to reel it back, pay attention to my nostrils.

264
00:26:14,564 --> 00:26:16,646
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the mind, which is already peripheral.

265
00:26:16,666 --> 00:26:18,267
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what needs to be pulled back.

266
00:26:18,947 --> 00:26:26,713
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that theme that you're kind of operating in, that's kind of driving your actions,

267
00:26:27,943 --> 00:26:31,085
[SPEAKER_04]: That is kind of too far out, so to speak.

268
00:26:31,926 --> 00:26:39,430
[SPEAKER_04]: And you need to bring that theme back to something a bit closer, like the body, for instance, or the feeling that you're acting out of.

269
00:26:39,490 --> 00:26:52,398
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that is... Well, feeling is a good example, because, like, that is always going to be... like... that's always going to be part of the theme of what you're acting out on.

270
00:26:52,858 --> 00:26:56,601
[SPEAKER_04]: So, say your theme is just, like, total central indulgence.

271
00:26:57,182 --> 00:27:03,464
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so can there be an attitude of delight towards what the senses present and at the same time reigning the mind?

272
00:27:03,484 --> 00:27:04,425
[SPEAKER_05]: Would that be possible?

273
00:27:22,750 --> 00:27:23,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is that a contradiction?

274
00:27:23,751 --> 00:27:29,573
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, things could be felt pleasantly, but it's different from delight, as this does talk about it.

275
00:27:30,173 --> 00:27:37,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by not welcoming and delighting what the senses present, then at least the mind doesn't flow in that direction, say.

276
00:27:37,815 --> 00:27:41,136
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's also then the opposite, which is the aversion, wanting to resist it.

277
00:27:41,376 --> 00:27:41,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

278
00:27:41,816 --> 00:27:45,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the attitude of not welcoming and not resisting,

279
00:27:47,459 --> 00:27:51,762
[SPEAKER_00]: That is how the mind is not moving along with what the senses is doing.

280
00:27:52,523 --> 00:27:56,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Which seems to me, which is the reigning, and it doesn't need to go that much further.

281
00:27:57,467 --> 00:28:03,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Because otherwise, you would be... if you go further than that, you would... you can sort of react into what the senses do, right?

282
00:28:03,872 --> 00:28:07,675
[SPEAKER_01]: You go too far, so that's what I said, you neither push forward, nor do you stand still.

283
00:28:07,755 --> 00:28:09,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so... And...

284
00:28:10,157 --> 00:28:10,517
[SPEAKER_01]: But then...

285
00:28:11,138 --> 00:28:12,699
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is it that's dangerous?

286
00:28:13,960 --> 00:28:15,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it the sense objects?

287
00:28:18,385 --> 00:28:19,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's always good to emphasize.

288
00:28:20,166 --> 00:28:21,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is the actual danger here?

289
00:28:28,470 --> 00:28:34,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, since objects in and of themselves would be dangerous, we would basically be lost, as far as this practice goes.

290
00:28:35,165 --> 00:28:44,227
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the danger is precisely in losing one's mindfulness, and when you lose it, you will just be by default acting out.

291
00:28:44,947 --> 00:28:46,587
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the danger.

292
00:28:47,028 --> 00:28:57,650
[SPEAKER_00]: But when that mindfulness is right, which includes... which is not a mechanical technique, but which includes... which is precisely the recollection of the danger, that's the mindfulness, which will prevent you from

293
00:28:58,310 --> 00:28:58,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Acting out.

294
00:28:58,810 --> 00:29:04,993
[SPEAKER_00]: If you see a poisonous snake in the room, you're not just going to play around with it, because you put your life at risk.

295
00:29:05,493 --> 00:29:13,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to keep an eye on it, observe its movements, where is it, but you don't want to come too close, nor do you want to forget about it.

296
00:29:14,096 --> 00:29:14,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Right?

297
00:29:14,796 --> 00:29:21,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Yeah, so that's why the Buddha said the six senses... Actually, there was this one sutta where Mahārāja comes to the Buddha and says,

298
00:29:22,435 --> 00:29:23,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything belongs to me.

299
00:29:23,556 --> 00:29:28,500
[SPEAKER_01]: The eye, the ear, the nose, the tongue, the body, the mind.

300
00:29:29,321 --> 00:29:30,482
[SPEAKER_01]: A consciousness belongs to me.

301
00:29:30,742 --> 00:29:31,823
[SPEAKER_01]: The five areas belong to me.

302
00:29:31,863 --> 00:29:32,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything belongs to me.

303
00:29:32,784 --> 00:29:33,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Where will you escape?

304
00:29:35,528 --> 00:29:37,670
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the point.

305
00:29:37,690 --> 00:29:46,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if your practice relies on just getting a different thing, even if it's just on the mental level, you're still resting on manas domain.

306
00:29:47,638 --> 00:29:48,439
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not samatha.

307
00:29:48,459 --> 00:29:52,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Samatha is... even samatha is like you go outside of it, temporarily, of course.

308
00:29:53,263 --> 00:29:53,764
[SPEAKER_01]: But, eh...

309
00:29:55,023 --> 00:29:58,545
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the danger is the mental attitude.

310
00:29:59,145 --> 00:30:01,446
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is not that there's a trap there.

311
00:30:02,287 --> 00:30:04,288
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that you are stepping on it.

312
00:30:05,368 --> 00:30:11,191
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point of the first jhāna, where it says you separate.

313
00:30:11,987 --> 00:30:14,968
[SPEAKER_01]: You basically separate from the baits, or separate from the traps.

314
00:30:15,248 --> 00:30:17,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that they're not there, but you don't bite them anymore.

315
00:30:17,489 --> 00:30:24,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it says the five senses are still there, but there is freedom within that.

316
00:30:24,552 --> 00:30:27,313
[SPEAKER_01]: There's freedom within that which used to be a trap.

317
00:30:28,319 --> 00:30:34,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, further development of samatha is not like in the direction of, okay, now I absorb myself.

318
00:30:35,003 --> 00:30:36,644
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same contemplation of danger.

319
00:30:37,624 --> 00:30:47,150
[SPEAKER_01]: As it is to say, once you've studied in the first jhāna, you reflect on the affliction in the same way you reflect on the affliction of the the five hundred siddhas in Shravasti.

320
00:30:47,190 --> 00:30:51,433
[SPEAKER_01]: You reflect on the affliction of the first jhāna, which is now obviously not unwholesome.

321
00:30:52,193 --> 00:30:57,406
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's just like less pleasant than not even having that there, like the thinking and pondering.

322
00:30:57,426 --> 00:30:59,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so you might see something beyond the first jhāna.

323
00:31:00,761 --> 00:31:02,182
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, which would be the second jhāna.

324
00:31:02,222 --> 00:31:02,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

325
00:31:02,623 --> 00:31:08,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, then, you know, you wouldn't even need to predetermine or know anything about what the second jhāna is.

326
00:31:08,587 --> 00:31:14,392
[SPEAKER_01]: You just develop the same principle in relation to this thing that you have now.

327
00:31:15,053 --> 00:31:16,274
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you would go further.

328
00:31:16,754 --> 00:31:17,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And you just keep doing that.

329
00:31:17,795 --> 00:31:21,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, as to say, eventually, you would reach the cessation of perception and feeling.

330
00:31:21,398 --> 00:31:24,981
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see, even even to deepen the samatha, you still need to contemplate.

331
00:31:25,401 --> 00:31:26,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And as to say that, when

332
00:31:29,364 --> 00:31:38,267
[SPEAKER_01]: He says he reflects like this, and then his mind becomes established in that new dimension, quote-unquote, because that's what pulls the mind back.

333
00:31:38,527 --> 00:31:45,510
[SPEAKER_01]: It's this clarity of this thing that's unwanted, and therefore the mind doesn't want anything to do with it, even though it's still there.

334
00:31:45,850 --> 00:31:46,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

335
00:31:51,177 --> 00:32:17,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And in another sutta, I already mentioned the one that says walking, sitting, standing during jhāna, but then there's another one that says, if a monk, his mindfulness is imperturbable, his effort is unwavering, his body is calm, his mind is composed, while walking,

336
00:32:18,053 --> 00:32:21,076
[SPEAKER_01]: He's said to be diligent, ardent and resolute.

337
00:32:21,957 --> 00:32:29,884
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, his body is calm while walking, which for most people would seem like a contradiction, because they associate

338
00:32:30,686 --> 00:32:53,931
[SPEAKER_01]: The word samatha, which, yes, could be translated as stillness, with, like, stillness of movement, but stillness of movement is not the same as calmness of mind, or, like, freedom from defilements, which is what matters, because, I think I might have mentioned that before, like, a sniper is very still when he's about to shoot someone, but that doesn't mean...

339
00:32:54,191 --> 00:32:57,392
[SPEAKER_01]: But on the level of defilements, he's not actually still.

340
00:32:58,332 --> 00:33:04,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot equate just lack of movement with wholesomeness.

341
00:33:05,155 --> 00:33:13,819
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much more subtle than that, and that's why, yeah, you need to first understand what needs to be calmed down.

342
00:33:13,999 --> 00:33:20,222
[SPEAKER_01]: As it says, you need to see the signs in your mind, because that's where you see what the problem is, and then you can calm down.

343
00:33:20,522 --> 00:33:20,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

344
00:33:28,402 --> 00:33:44,195
[SPEAKER_03]: So, from that discussion, when we started talking about the... seeing the danger, it came across that it was just simply seeing... seeing the state of your mind, which is the extent necessary.

345
00:33:45,035 --> 00:33:48,378
[SPEAKER_03]: If you go too far, and try to...

346
00:33:50,780 --> 00:33:56,645
[SPEAKER_03]: I can't think of an example, but... that would be the middle.

347
00:33:58,335 --> 00:33:59,856
[SPEAKER_03]: The middle ground.

348
00:33:59,876 --> 00:34:09,866
[SPEAKER_03]: As soon as you start bringing that state of mind into the focus, trying to start playing with it, you've gone too far.

349
00:34:11,447 --> 00:34:18,974
[SPEAKER_03]: So, is it just then... because that then reminds me of

350
00:34:19,327 --> 00:34:24,330
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, what is usually not emphasized in that, is that you have a responsibility for acting out.

351
00:34:38,579 --> 00:34:40,901
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where hindrances are, right?

352
00:34:40,941 --> 00:34:46,324
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the presence of a sensual thought or an angry thought in and of itself that constitutes a hindrance.

353
00:34:47,005 --> 00:34:50,888
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you going towards it or wanting to move away from it.

354
00:34:50,988 --> 00:34:56,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what needs to be discerned and that's not... that usually doesn't get sufficient

355
00:34:57,652 --> 00:34:58,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, it's not mentioned at all.

356
00:34:59,253 --> 00:35:03,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when people say, you know, like, church is awareness, or things like this.

357
00:35:03,314 --> 00:35:07,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but actually, you are choosing, and you cannot not choose.

358
00:35:07,495 --> 00:35:11,476
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to incorporate that dimension as well.

359
00:35:12,216 --> 00:35:16,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, I don't see how you can move beyond those indices.

360
00:35:17,098 --> 00:35:17,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and also...

361
00:35:18,418 --> 00:35:23,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first of all, maybe you could say to Sotāpanna, just be aware, because he knows what that means.

362
00:35:23,540 --> 00:35:24,180
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing is,

363
00:35:25,979 --> 00:35:33,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody who hasn't understood what wholesome and unwholesome is, their idea of being aware might not be what they actually need to be doing.

364
00:35:34,021 --> 00:35:42,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, by just mindlessly sticking to that, they would essentially omit the work that they need to do to get to stream entry.

365
00:35:42,402 --> 00:35:47,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the suttas don't say you get to stream entry by through meditation.

366
00:35:47,463 --> 00:35:51,384
[SPEAKER_01]: You basically get to stream entry by understanding how to meditate.

367
00:35:52,845 --> 00:35:53,846
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when the path begins.

368
00:35:54,146 --> 00:35:56,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you gain the seven enlightenment factors.

369
00:35:56,268 --> 00:36:06,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's more like... yeah, to some extent, you will have some... your recognition of these mental states will be improving.

370
00:36:07,570 --> 00:36:25,585
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're practicing the prerequisites of sense restraint, virtue, and these things, and reflecting honestly with the recognition that you still don't see the full extent of it, and if at no point you become complacent with thinking, okay, I understood now what greed, aversion, and delusion are, what the hindrances are,

371
00:36:26,585 --> 00:36:29,627
[SPEAKER_01]: It's uncomfortable, but you keep the context that there's still more to it.

372
00:36:29,767 --> 00:36:31,028
[SPEAKER_01]: There still has to be more to it.

373
00:36:31,949 --> 00:36:38,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, at some point, well, you will see the full extent of what unwholesome things are, and that's the right view.

374
00:36:39,714 --> 00:36:48,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as you said, like, putting it into... making it into the foreground, when you're trying to discern the mental state, the thing is the mental state can never be the foreground.

375
00:36:48,719 --> 00:36:48,920
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

376
00:36:49,886 --> 00:36:52,329
[SPEAKER_01]: When we're talking about it theoretically, it sounds obvious.

377
00:36:52,369 --> 00:36:54,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, sure, there's the mental state and then there's the sense object.

378
00:36:55,072 --> 00:36:59,497
[SPEAKER_01]: But practically speaking, there will always be a tendency to confuse the two.

379
00:36:59,797 --> 00:37:06,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you think that the way the sense object feels is the attitude of the mind towards it.

380
00:37:08,354 --> 00:37:10,156
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's a line between those two.

381
00:37:10,176 --> 00:37:29,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as the suttas say, you don't... Actually, it talks about the five senses in the first jhāna, and it says, amidst things, sights cognizable by the eye, sounds cognizable by the ear, and so on, that are likable, agreeable, pleasant, provocative of lust,

382
00:37:30,909 --> 00:37:35,411
[SPEAKER_01]: There is an escape in the midst of those things, an opening amidst that confinement.

383
00:37:35,451 --> 00:37:38,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not even that you change the way that sense objects feel.

384
00:37:40,333 --> 00:37:46,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Other suttas would say it's like, you're dispassionate amidst that which incites passion.

385
00:37:46,776 --> 00:37:49,418
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to then realize that...

386
00:37:51,145 --> 00:38:06,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that mental state is really what needs to be... the work is in being able to draw that line between that which has arisen on its own, which is the sense object, including the way it feels, because that also belongs to Mara, as it is to say.

387
00:38:07,570 --> 00:38:09,851
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, what you're doing in regard to it.

388
00:38:10,492 --> 00:38:23,799
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why then, as we always keep repeating, it starts with virtue, because it's on that same level of me when I see something that I want to steal with my hand, and I do it.

389
00:38:24,359 --> 00:38:33,284
[SPEAKER_01]: See, it's not my fault that the thing presented itself as nice, as something I would like to have, but it is my responsibility if I go and take it.

390
00:38:33,988 --> 00:38:35,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same principle on the mental level.

391
00:38:35,689 --> 00:38:42,351
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your choice at all whether the thing presents itself as agreeable, likable, etc.

392
00:38:42,932 --> 00:38:44,732
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your choice when you take it up.

393
00:38:44,952 --> 00:38:47,113
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's that taking up that you have to recognize.

394
00:38:47,553 --> 00:38:51,715
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's that taking up that you have to contemplate as dangerous, not the thing that came up.

395
00:38:52,195 --> 00:39:00,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if, like, if I'm not going to steal anything, then, I mean, you can present me, like, a case of... a briefcase of two million dollars.

396
00:39:00,379 --> 00:39:01,599
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not dangerous in itself.

397
00:39:01,639 --> 00:39:03,580
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dangerous when I steal it and then I go to jail.

398
00:39:05,509 --> 00:39:06,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, of course.

399
00:39:06,411 --> 00:39:13,041
[SPEAKER_00]: The thing is, you contemplate the danger and so on, but you always keep it at the back of your mind that it's highly likely that

400
00:39:22,237 --> 00:39:27,424
[SPEAKER_01]: There's still more to that mental state that you still haven't seen.

401
00:39:27,805 --> 00:39:33,152
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't assume like, okay, I see now what the lust is and now it's just about contemplating the danger.

402
00:39:33,653 --> 00:39:36,677
[SPEAKER_01]: You exercise like...

403
00:39:37,418 --> 00:39:47,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplating the danger, but you always keep in mind that you may not be, at any given time, you can make the mistake of falling back into contemplating sense objects, instead of contemplating the mind.

404
00:39:48,441 --> 00:39:53,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, as I always give the example of, like, somebody who's learning how to lift weights.

405
00:39:54,703 --> 00:39:58,664
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't just get focused now on piling up the weight and lifting as much as possible.

406
00:39:59,324 --> 00:40:02,625
[SPEAKER_01]: They always keep in mind that, as a beginner,

407
00:40:03,846 --> 00:40:05,247
[SPEAKER_01]: They can have bad form.

408
00:40:05,488 --> 00:40:07,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can start making wrong movements.

409
00:40:08,452 --> 00:40:13,258
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just about, like, becoming complacent with, okay, this is how you lift the weight and now just doing it.

410
00:40:13,999 --> 00:40:17,203
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always watching out for how you're doing it, why you're doing it.

411
00:40:17,809 --> 00:40:20,750
[SPEAKER_01]: And as we always say, the intention is behind your actions.

412
00:40:21,310 --> 00:40:24,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can be contemplating danger out of sensual reasons.

413
00:40:25,911 --> 00:40:32,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you want some experience of release, of something like that, and then you fail to see that that is the hindrance.

414
00:40:33,033 --> 00:40:37,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're contemplating danger because you want something.

415
00:40:37,794 --> 00:40:41,094
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't see... Because you might want something more refined and sensual.

416
00:40:41,134 --> 00:40:41,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

417
00:40:41,595 --> 00:40:43,775
[SPEAKER_01]: You want something outside of this experience.

418
00:40:44,295 --> 00:40:47,056
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the actual thing that you should see as dangerous.

419
00:40:48,036 --> 00:40:55,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, like I said, you fall onto the level of sense objects, or you could say sense experiences, even the most subtle phenomena.

420
00:40:56,499 --> 00:41:00,801
[SPEAKER_01]: You're craving for them, and then you fail to see, is the craving in relation to them?

421
00:41:01,141 --> 00:41:02,001
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem.

422
00:41:02,021 --> 00:41:07,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like whether I don't have this thing now, that I think I should have.

423
00:41:07,423 --> 00:41:07,543
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

424
00:41:07,603 --> 00:41:13,085
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like you get the right experience, which is the right calm and everything, by not wanting.

425
00:41:13,365 --> 00:41:14,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And that not wanting takes effort.

426
00:41:15,377 --> 00:41:17,197
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes discernment, it takes practice.

427
00:41:17,357 --> 00:41:20,058
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just a choiceless awareness that you relax into.

428
00:41:20,738 --> 00:41:24,299
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to understand what it means to not want things.

429
00:41:26,919 --> 00:41:31,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So, crave to understand how not to crave.

430
00:41:32,060 --> 00:41:35,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Not crave for some experience that you justify as wholesome.

431
00:41:36,981 --> 00:41:40,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And any experience that then comes out of complete non-craving,

432
00:41:41,532 --> 00:41:50,557
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like you don't even need to ask yourself whether this is wholesome, or hear it from somebody else, or you don't even need to debate, like, which is the right jhāna, which is not the right jhāna.

433
00:41:52,338 --> 00:41:53,839
[SPEAKER_01]: If there's no craving, that's right.

434
00:41:54,199 --> 00:41:54,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

435
00:41:55,280 --> 00:41:58,782
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how there's the craving that brings about the end of craving.

436
00:41:58,922 --> 00:41:59,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

437
00:41:59,302 --> 00:41:59,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

438
00:42:01,159 --> 00:42:04,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, that doesn't mean you would have jhāna, eh, craving, inside.

439
00:42:04,980 --> 00:42:06,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, samatha is the coming of craving.

440
00:42:06,881 --> 00:42:09,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that you have, like, craving within these...

441
00:42:10,102 --> 00:42:12,223
[SPEAKER_01]: When you succeed, that's exactly when.

442
00:42:12,443 --> 00:42:13,204
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, but you have...

443
00:42:13,224 --> 00:42:14,264
[SPEAKER_01]: But one has to start somewhere.

444
00:42:14,304 --> 00:42:15,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, of course, yeah.

445
00:42:15,985 --> 00:42:19,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, just clarifying, because... Then, you just see exactly the same thing.

446
00:42:19,766 --> 00:42:24,228
[SPEAKER_01]: You think on the level of, like, what is the right type of experience for me to have?

447
00:42:24,928 --> 00:42:28,189
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't consider that it doesn't matter what experience it is.

448
00:42:28,209 --> 00:42:29,589
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is that there's no craving.

449
00:42:29,609 --> 00:42:30,350
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what makes it false.

450
00:42:30,370 --> 00:42:30,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

451
00:42:30,750 --> 00:42:32,210
[SPEAKER_00]: That's perhaps good to emphasize.

452
00:42:32,230 --> 00:42:38,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, craving does not necessarily need to be towards this or that particular thing.

453
00:42:38,412 --> 00:42:41,213
[SPEAKER_00]: One can crave experience itself.

454
00:42:41,473 --> 00:42:41,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

455
00:42:41,793 --> 00:42:43,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is also a form of craving.

456
00:42:43,173 --> 00:42:43,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

457
00:42:43,973 --> 00:42:47,634
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it says, eh... Craving is that which delights here and there.

458
00:42:47,915 --> 00:42:49,055
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it doesn't say it's...

459
00:42:49,775 --> 00:42:55,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Something specific that, like, this thing is craving, and then, if you're not doing that specific thing, then you're free from craving.

460
00:42:55,299 --> 00:42:56,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that was far more general.

461
00:42:56,761 --> 00:43:01,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's anything within the six, within the all, within the six sense spaces.

462
00:43:01,284 --> 00:43:01,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

463
00:43:02,085 --> 00:43:03,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you find the escape within that.

464
00:43:03,966 --> 00:43:11,472
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's actually a very, very lofty achievement to even reach samatha.

465
00:43:11,592 --> 00:43:18,798
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, like, you know, everybody in the Buddhist time was, was already doing that, and it's just like any...

466
00:43:20,263 --> 00:43:20,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

467
00:43:20,503 --> 00:43:22,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Within five days, you get it or something.

468
00:43:22,384 --> 00:43:22,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

469
00:43:22,684 --> 00:43:23,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you do it.

470
00:43:23,184 --> 00:43:23,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

471
00:43:23,424 --> 00:43:23,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

472
00:43:23,864 --> 00:43:28,186
[SPEAKER_00]: You do a five-day meditation retreat, and from day one, people instruct you in samatha, vipassana.

473
00:43:28,226 --> 00:43:28,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

474
00:43:28,626 --> 00:43:30,227
[SPEAKER_00]: That's actually seriously misleading.

475
00:43:31,127 --> 00:43:34,408
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to say, the way to the deathless is samatha.

476
00:43:35,488 --> 00:43:37,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the next, the way to the deathless, vipassana.

477
00:43:37,969 --> 00:43:38,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

478
00:43:39,750 --> 00:43:39,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

479
00:44:01,093 --> 00:44:07,154
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you could crave for understanding, if that's synonymous with the ending of craving.

480
00:44:08,235 --> 00:44:08,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

481
00:44:09,175 --> 00:44:22,818
[SPEAKER_01]: If you keep in mind that, like, see, the issue with this idea of craving, or the end of craving, is that you shouldn't confuse

482
00:44:24,382 --> 00:44:28,824
[SPEAKER_01]: You will have a general sort of desire to understand, and you don't give that up.

483
00:44:29,584 --> 00:44:53,132
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't justify acting out of restlessness, and doubt, and like... No, you see, yes, I have this general chanda, as to say, like, interest in understanding, but you don't justify all sorts of other unwholesome states in the name of, you know, craving for the end of craving.

484
00:44:54,512 --> 00:44:59,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because then, that's more like just replacing one hindrance with the other.

485
00:45:01,076 --> 00:45:11,181
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, if you're imagining... if you have some picture of what this understanding will look like in your experience, and you're craving after that.

486
00:45:13,282 --> 00:45:18,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, if you're thinking, again, in terms of like some experience that I want to get,

487
00:45:19,925 --> 00:45:26,247
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you should think of it more as in how do I clarify non-craving in regard to any experience.

488
00:45:26,767 --> 00:45:32,028
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you understand that, the more you... it will make sense that actually it kind of doesn't make sense to pray for it.

489
00:45:32,548 --> 00:45:35,669
[SPEAKER_01]: But initially, you have to start with what you have, which is craving.

490
00:45:36,209 --> 00:45:41,491
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you understand it, you see like it is... like nibbāna isn't something that you can pray for.

491
00:45:42,271 --> 00:45:47,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not an experience in terms of like something that comes to you.

492
00:45:48,475 --> 00:45:50,837
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an attitude in relation to whatever is already there.

493
00:45:51,477 --> 00:45:55,800
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you understand it properly, that's why it says the dhamma doesn't take time.

494
00:45:56,901 --> 00:45:59,643
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're thinking in terms of like, oh, this thing I will get later.

495
00:46:00,384 --> 00:46:11,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, you start with that, but eventually you realize like, no, if I had understood this principle from day one, there wouldn't have been anything stopping me from understanding what nibbāna is.

496
00:46:13,956 --> 00:46:18,322
[SPEAKER_00]: It would then just be a question of having a sufficiently strong mind to not fall away from that, basically.

497
00:46:18,342 --> 00:46:18,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

498
00:46:18,943 --> 00:46:22,208
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that, basically, nobody really starts with that kind of strong mind.

499
00:46:22,228 --> 00:46:23,109
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it takes time.

500
00:46:23,349 --> 00:46:23,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

501
00:46:23,670 --> 00:46:25,893
[SPEAKER_01]: But the principle in itself is timeless.

502
00:46:25,993 --> 00:46:26,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

503
00:46:26,514 --> 00:46:27,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's it.

504
00:46:27,455 --> 00:46:27,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

505
00:46:27,576 --> 00:46:27,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

506
00:46:29,883 --> 00:46:40,469
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why then you would have people who would achieve arhantship in one night, others in two days, others in seven days, up to seven years.

507
00:46:40,529 --> 00:46:46,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just the same principle, but that is timeless.

508
00:46:47,493 --> 00:46:50,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's just a matter of like...

509
00:46:51,159 --> 00:46:54,541
[SPEAKER_01]: How long it takes for the mind to become settled in it, so that it doesn't fall away.

510
00:46:54,761 --> 00:46:55,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

511
00:46:55,402 --> 00:46:58,564
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not that they were like building up this nibbāna energy or something.

512
00:46:58,764 --> 00:46:58,844
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

513
00:46:59,324 --> 00:47:07,569
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like... understanding it, so that you don't fall away from it, even in the most... adverse circumstances.

514
00:47:07,649 --> 00:47:09,090
